{
  "rationale": "Evidence: all eight prior designs went off track in all 60 trials per design. max_error sat at 0.061 to 0.066 m, just past the 0.06 m limit, whatever the gain (kp 1.2 to 3), speed (0.6 to 1.6), sensor span (+/-0.03 to +/-0.06 m), mass (0.4 to 0.8 kg) or ki/kd. Progress was nearly fixed by track (oval about 0.12 to 0.17, s-bend about 0.04 to 0.07). Iteration 7 and 8 narrowed the sensor span, which cut line-loss events to about 1 per run, but did not change the failure point. Controller sweeps have stopped moving the outcome, and iteration 7's own notes asked for a change in drivetrain geometry if nothing moved.\n\nDiagnosis: a failure this insensitive to gain suggests the robot lacks steering authority, not that it oscillates. Yaw rate is (right wheel speed minus left) divided by wheel_track, so a wide track (0.14 m) makes each unit of PWM difference produce less turn. The robot may simply cannot turn fast enough at the first bend, whatever the gain.\n\nChanges from iteration 8: (1) wheel_track reduced from 0.14 to 0.09 m, which raises yaw rate per unit wheel difference by about 55 percent. This is the main test. (2) kp raised from 1.2 to 2.0 to use the extra authority, with the same signal scale (weights x/0.04). (3) kd lowered from 0.6 to 0.5 to keep damping in proportion to the higher gain. (4) ki kept at 0.3 to cancel the motor mismatch, with the integral bounded at +/-2. (5) base_speed raised slightly from 0.6 to 0.8 so the robot covers more track, since the steering change is meant to keep it on the line at that speed. Sensor geometry (7 sensors, x = -0.03 to +0.03, y = 0.06, 12 mm), wheel_radius 0.03, mass 0.4 and body are unchanged, so the wheel_track effect can be read directly. line_loss stays last_direction.\n\nExpected result: if steering authority was the limit, max_error should fall below about 0.05 m and progress should rise above 0.2 on the oval and s-bend. Completion within 3 s is still unlikely. If max_error stays near 0.06 with the narrow track, steering authority is not the limit, and the next draft should test wheel_radius or a larger change in base_speed.",
